Do not treat a government bond yield as equivalent to a bond futures basis factor.

Only execute an HDS query if sufficient query budget remains.

## 8. MAPPING VALIDATION REGISTRY

For each investigated factor, record:

| Field | Description |
|---|---|
| Excel factor | Scenario Manager identifier |
| HDS factor ID | Actual HDS numeric identifier |
| HDS factor name | Exact name from HDS |
| HDS source ticker | Source market identifier |
| HDS source system | Source system |
| Frequency | Verified frequency |
| Market definition | Underlying instrument |
| Unit | Price, percentage, bps, etc. |
| Quote convention | Direction and interpretation |
| Mapping type | Direct, derived or proxy |
| Evidence | Actual query results and reference evidence |
| Status | Confirmed, candidate, proxy or unmapped |
| Blockers | Missing metadata or validation |
| Next action | Exact requirement for confirmation |

Never populate identifiers from assumptions.

Maintain a clear distinction between:

- Observed HDS facts
- Inferred candidate relationships
- Validated mappings
- Unresolved issues

## 9. LIMITED HISTORICAL VERIFICATION

If you identify a strong candidate and have remaining authorized query budget, perform one small historical verification.

Retrieve at most 10 observations using:

- Exact market-factor ID.
- Confirmed partition.
- Relevant frequency.
- Narrow historical date window.
- Selected required columns.

Check whether:

- Observations are genuinely historical.
- Date and level columns are populated.
- Units and price conventions are plausible.
- Multiple runs or snapshots create duplicates.
- Daily observations are available.

Do not calculate shock percentiles from a 10-row sample.

Do not retrieve the full history until the mapping and data-extraction approach are approved.

